class Solution {
    public int findMin(int[] nums) {
        int start = 0;                  // starting index
        int end = nums.length - 1;      // ending index

        // Binary search loop
        while (start < end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;  // avoid overflow

            // If middle element is greater than the last element,
            // it means the minimum is in the right half
            if (nums[mid] > nums[end]) {
                start = mid + 1;
            } 
            // Otherwise, the minimum is in the left half (including mid)
            else {
                end = mid;
            }
        }

        // When loop ends, start == end → pointing to minimum element
        return nums[start];
    }
}
🧠 Intuition (Important)
The array is sorted + rotated
One half is always sorted
We use nums[end] to decide which side contains the minimum
⏱ Time Complexity
O(log n)
Because we halve the search space every iteration (binary search)
💾 Space Complexity
O(1)
No extra space used (in-place variables only)
